.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKM-CREATE-CERTIFICATE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekm-create-certificate \- generate and store a self-signed certificate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekm create-certificate
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-common\-name\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fIHOST\fR]
[\fB\-\-alt\-name\fR|\fB\-a\fR \fIHOST\fR]...
[\fB\-\-valid\-days\fR|\fB\-v\fR \fIDAYS\fR]
[\fB\-\-key\-bits\fR|\fB\-b\fR \fIBITS\fR]
[\fB\-\-description\fR|\fB\-d\fR \fITEXT\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Generates a self-signed certificate and stores it under a name, for an
installation that has to serve HTTPS before anybody has bought it a
real certificate: development, a demonstration, an internal network
where the clients can be told what to trust.
.PP
Nobody has vouched for the result. A client rejects it until it is
given the certificate as something to trust \- which for
.B euclid-cli
itself is what
.B \-\-ca\-cert
does. Fetch the certificate to hand to one with
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-get-certificate (1).
.PP
Give every name callers will use. A client checks the name it dialled
against the certificate's subject alternative names and refuses the
connection if it is not among them; the common name is always included
as one, and
.B \-\-alt\-name
adds the rest. A value that parses as an IP address is stored as one,
which is what makes a certificate work for a caller dialling
.I 127.0.0.1
rather than a host name.
.PP
An API gateway listener that speaks HTTPS does this for itself when its
certificate does not exist yet, so this action is for the cases where
the name, the validity period or the key length should be something
other than what the gateway would have chosen.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Name the certificate is stored under. Required.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-common\-name " " \fIHOST\fR
Subject common name, normally the host name callers use. Defaults to
the certificate's own name.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-alt\-name " " \fIHOST\fR
A further host name or IP address the certificate is valid for. May be
given more than once.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-valid\-days " " \fIDAYS\fR
How many days the certificate is valid for. Defaults to \fI825\fR.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-key\-bits " " \fIBITS\fR
RSA key length. Defaults to \fI2048\fR.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-description " " \fITEXT\fR
What the certificate is for. Optional.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The certificate was generated and stored; its name, subject,
fingerprint and validity period are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing/invalid arguments, generation failed, the server could not be
reached, or it rejected the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint https://localhost:5566 ekm create-certificate \e
    --name eag-development \e
    --common-name api.internal \e
    --alt-name localhost --alt-name 127.0.0.1 \e
    --valid-days 365
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-import-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-list-certificates (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-get-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-certificate (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag (1)
